expected_issues.description:
`contains("timings")` only checks the key exists; it does not guarantee the value is an object. Using `at("timings")` can throw if `timings` is null/non-object, potentially aborting the whole RPC. Prefer `find()` + `is_object()` (and reuse the reference) to make this robust and avoid double lookup.

```suggestion
                const auto it = result.result_json.find("timings");
                if (it != result.result_json.end() && it->is_object()) {
                    const auto& timings = *it;
                    reply.set_timing_prompt_processing(timings.value("prompt_ms", 0.0));
                    reply.set_timing_token_generation(timings.value("predicted_ms", 0.0));
                }
```

